  <article class="article">
    <h1>Safety</h1>
    <p><em>Last updated: 2026-10-23 · Applies to incenseherbs.com and data.incenseherbs.com</em></p>
    <p class="lead">
      Incense is a burning material. Used responsibly, it is a pleasant aromatic practice; used
      carelessly, it presents real risks. This page is our transparent safety disclosure, covering
      open flame, smoke, product forms, storage, first aid, and material-specific cautions.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Incense involves open flame</h2>
    <p>
      Most forms of incense are lit with an open flame and continue to smoulder after ignition. Treat
      a lit incense item like any other burning object. The basic rules apply to every form on this
      site:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Burn in a <strong>well-ventilated</strong> space.</li>
      <li>Place the item on a <strong>heat-safe, non-flammable holder or burner</strong> on a stable,
        level surface, away from anything that can catch fire — curtains, paper, fabric, foliage.</li>
      <li><strong>Never leave burning or smouldering incense unattended</strong> — including when you
        leave the room, sleep, or leave the house.</li>
      <li>Keep lit incense, lighters, and matches <strong>away from children and pets</strong>.</li>
      <li>Keep a source of water or a fire extinguisher available, and know your local fire-safety
        rules.</li>
      <li>Allow ash and the holder to <strong>cool completely before disposing</strong> — ash can stay
        hot and ignite refuse.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Before you light anything, confirm you have: a heat-safe holder that fits the form you are
      burning, a clear flat surface away from flammables and drafts, ventilation available (a window
      or door you can open), and the item in view for its whole burn. A draft from a window, fan, or
      air conditioner can carry embers or ash onto flammable surfaces — position the holder so falling
      ash lands on a non-combustible tray, never on carpet, wood, or paper. If something catches fire,
      extinguish it promptly with water or a suitable extinguisher; for a fire you cannot control,
      evacuate and call your local emergency number.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Smoke and respiratory health</h2>
    <p>
      Burning incense produces <strong>smoke and particulate matter</strong>. We do not claim incense
      smoke is harmless — combustion of any plant material produces particles you should not inhale in
      concentration. Ventilate the room, and avoid leaning directly over the smoke or inhaling it
      deeply.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you have a respiratory condition — such as asthma, COPD, or allergies — or a heart condition,
      if you are pregnant, or if you are generally sensitive to smoke, consult a qualified healthcare
      professional before burning incense indoors, and stop and seek medical advice if you experience
      breathing difficulty, coughing, wheezing, dizziness, or irritation. If a child or pet shows
      distress around incense smoke, remove them from the area and seek advice.
    </p>
    <p>
      Burn in moderation. No single session should fill the room with smoke, and continuous or
      frequent burning in an enclosed space raises your long-term exposure to particulate matter.
      Burn only as much as you need for the moment, extinguish the item when you are done rather than
      letting it smoulder out over hours, and air the space between sessions.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Warnings by product form</h2>
    <p>
      Different forms burn differently and carry different practical cautions. These are the forms
      covered on this site. If a product you hold is not one of these forms, follow its own label
      instructions.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Incense sticks (线香).</strong> A thin combustible core that burns steadily. Use an
        upright or lying holder made for the length, with a tray to catch falling ash; do not rest a
        burning stick on a combustible surface.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Incense coils (盘香).</strong> A spiral that burns for hours. Suspend or support the
        coil on the stand provided, over a heat-safe tray, and expect a long burn — do not leave it
        unattended for its whole duration.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Incense powder (香粉).</strong> Loose powder is often burned on a heat source or
        compressed into a trail. Use only the intended heat source and container; loose powder can
        ignite or smoulder unpredictably, and excess heat can scorch the burner.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Resins and gums (树脂).</strong> Resins such as frankincense are melted on a hot
        surface or charcoal. The burner, charcoal disc, and resin become extremely hot — do not touch
        during or immediately after burning, and never use a container that cannot withstand high heat.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cones (塔香) and wood chips (香木).</strong> Cones burn from the base upward and must
        sit on a non-flammable dish. Wood chips are usually heated rather than directly burned; use a
        proper heater and never leave it on unattended.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Several forms are <strong>heated</strong> rather than directly burned — resins and wood chips on
      a charcoal disc, an electric incense heater, or a mica-plate burner. These still reach very high
      temperatures. Follow the heater's instructions, place it on a heat-safe surface, keep it away
      from anything combustible, let it cool fully before touching or storing, and never leave an
      electric heater or a lit charcoal disc on unattended. Dispose of spent charcoal only after it is
      completely cold, and wet it down if in doubt.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Storage and contact safety</h2>
    <p>
      Store incense and all botanical materials in their original or clearly labelled containers, in a
      <strong>cool, dry place out of reach of children and pets</strong>, away from heat and direct
      sunlight, and separate from food so they cannot be mistaken for something to eat. Discard
      materials that have become mouldy, damp, or otherwise spoiled, and do not burn material you
      cannot identify.
    </p>
    <p><strong>If a material is swallowed:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Do not induce vomiting unless a poison-control centre or medical professional tells you to.</li>
      <li>Call your local poison-control centre or emergency services immediately, and tell them what
        was swallowed and roughly how much.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>If a material gets into the eyes:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Rinse the eye gently with clean, lukewarm water for several minutes.</li>
      <li>Remove contact lenses if present and easy to remove, and continue rinsing.</li>
      <li>Seek medical advice if irritation persists.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If a concentrated aromatic or essential oil contacts skin, wash the area with soap and water and
      stop use if irritation develops. If a material is inhaled heavily or causes breathing trouble,
      move to fresh air and seek medical advice. This is general first-aid guidance; it does not
      replace advice from a poison-control centre or a healthcare professional for your specific
      situation.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Individual sensitivity</h2>
    <p>
      People react differently to the same material. A substance that is mild for one person can
      trigger allergy, respiratory irritation, or skin sensitivity in another. If you have known
      allergies or sensitivities, start with a small amount, ventilate well, and discontinue use and
      seek advice if you react. Do not keep burning a material that irritates you in the hope of
      "getting used to it"; stop and switch to something else or ventilate more. You are responsible
      for your own use; see our <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a> for the limits of the
      information on this site.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Natural does not mean safe</h2>
    <p>
      A material being "natural" or "botanical" does <strong>not</strong> mean it is automatically
      safe to inhale, handle, or consume. Natural products can contain allergens, irritants, and — in
      a few cases documented in our encyclopedia — genuinely toxic compounds. "Natural" is a
      description of origin, not a safety rating. This is a distinction we hold ourselves to
      throughout the site. We do not make medical or health claims, and we do not describe incense as
      a treatment for any condition.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Traditional use vs medical use</h2>
    <p>
      Traditional and cultural uses of these materials (ritual, meditation, materia medica) are
      <strong>traditional knowledge</strong>, not modern clinical evidence. Do not use incense as a
      substitute for medical care, and do not ingest any material based on a traditional-medicine
      description without professional guidance. Historical dosages recorded in classical texts are
      historical record, not instructions for you to follow. See the <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a>
      for the full statement.
    </p>

    <h2>8. Materials with stronger caveats</h2>
    <p>
      A few materials in our ingredient encyclopedia carry a <strong>stronger safety caveat</strong>
      than the general smoke warning — enough that they should not be burned, ingested, or handled
      without reading their entry first:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/ingredients/lily-of-the-valley/">Lily of the valley</a> — poisonous (cardiac
        glycosides); must never be burned or ingested.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/ingredients/calamus/">Calamus</a> — many varieties' essential oil contains
        β-asarone, restricted in food use in several jurisdictions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/ingredients/asarum/">Asarum</a> — the birthwort family (Aristolochiaceae) carries
        documented chemical-safety cautions and dosing cautions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/ingredients/apricot-kernel/">Apricot kernel</a> — bitter kernels contain amygdalin,
        a cyanide precursor; not for casual consumption.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/ingredients/star-anise/">Star anise</a> — do not confuse with the toxic Japanese
        star anise (<em>Illicium anisatum</em>).
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/ingredients/camphor/">Camphor</a> and <a href="/ingredients/borneol/">borneol</a> —
        concentrated aromatics that are not for ingestion.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These caveats are reproduced from each material's own entry; this page is only the central
      index. The entry page remains the authoritative source for that material's safety note. If you
      are unsure of a material's identity or provenance, do not burn or handle it — treat an
      unidentified substance as potentially hazardous.
    </p>

    <h2>9. Where to find material-specific cautions</h2>
    <p>
      Every ingredient page in our encyclopedia ends with a Safety section specific to that material,
      and any material with a stronger caveat repeats it there. Before using a material for the first
      time, read its entry's Safety section — this central page is a summary and index, not a
      substitute for the individual note. If a material is not in our encyclopedia, we have no safety
      note for it, and you should not assume it is safe simply because it resembles one we cover.
    </p>

